$NetBSD$

* Each reported button press gets exactly one release.  libinput counts the
  presses of a button per seat (seat_button_count); KWin drops a press whose
  count is not 1 and a release whose count is not 0.  The backend passed on
  every WSCONS_EVENT_MOUSE_DOWN, so one press whose release did not arrive
  (the kernel event queue lost it) left the count one too high for good:
  the compositor held that button down, did not move the pointer focus and
  dropped all later presses and releases of that button until it was
  restarted.  Disabling the device did not help, because the next press and
  release had already cleared the "pressed" bit of the backend.
  Now a press of a button that is already down first reports the lost
  release, and a release of a button that is not down is dropped as before.
  The rule is a small function between the "wscons-button-state" markers,
  tested on the host (tests/button-pairing.sh).
* A removed device releases its held buttons and ends its gesture before
  LIBINPUT_EVENT_DEVICE_REMOVED: it cannot send the releases any more, and
  the seat count would keep the buttons down for the other devices.

